From: SJ Park <sj@kernel.org>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:58:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mm/damon/sysfs-schemes: hold damon_sysfs_lock in
 target_value_store()

damon_sysfs_add_quota_score() reads target_value twice.  Once to check
if the value is valid (non-zero), and again to pass the value to the
core layer.  Meanwhile, target_value can be set by the user via
target_value_store() without any synchronization.  As a result, if the
user sets target_value as zero between the two reads in
damon_sysfs_add_quota_score(), zero target_value can be passed to the
core layer.  It can result in divide-by-zero in mult_frac() call of
damos_quota_score().

The write require sysfs write permission.  Sane users wouldn't do such
racy writes.  That said, the user impact is very bad: Divide-by-zero.

Fix by holding damon_sysfs_lock in target_value_store() while saving the
value.  The read side, damon_sysfs_add_quota_score(), is already called
while hoding the same mutex lock, so it avoids the race.

Closes: https://sashiko.dev/#/bug/linux-7f814c9a-986f-4103-a55a-ef9537dcf62b
Fixes: 8b549a4fd3c5 ("mm/damon/sysfs-schemes: commit damos quota goals user input to DAMOS")
Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org> # 6.8.x
Signed-off-by: SJ Park <sj@kernel.org>
---
 mm/damon/sysfs-schemes.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mm/damon/sysfs-schemes.c b/mm/damon/sysfs-schemes.c
index 3252e2ab09173..7ec98fbf7f6a5 100644
--- a/mm/damon/sysfs-schemes.c
+++ b/mm/damon/sysfs-schemes.c
@@ -1355,7 +1355,13 @@ static ssize_t target_value_store(struct kobject *kobj,
 {
 	struct damos_sysfs_quota_goal *goal = container_of(kobj, struct
 			damos_sysfs_quota_goal, kobj);
-	int err = kstrtoul(buf, 0, &goal->target_value);
+	unsigned long target_value;
+	int err = kstrtoul(buf, 0, &target_value);
+
+	if (!mutex_trylock(&damon_sysfs_lock))
+		return -EBUSY;
+	goal->target_value = target_value;
+	mutex_unlock(&damon_sysfs_lock);
 
 	return err ? err : count;
 }
